            <ArticleTitle>Persistent Left Superior Vena Cava Draining Into the Left Atrium Found During Bypass Operation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e161-e162</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0003-4975(16)30875-X</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.athoracsur.2016.06.106</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report the case of a 72-year-old woman who underwent urgent coronary bypass grafting (CABG). Intraoperatively a persistent left superior vena cava (PLSVC) draining into the left atrium was detected. Because of the resulting right-to-left shunt volume even after complete drainage and collapse of the right atrium, the heart was still ejecting in the absence of significant aortic insufficiency. Thorough examination revealed a PLSVC draining into the left atrium. After ligation of the PLSVC, the CABG could be performed as planned.</AbstractText>
